

Simple Linear Regression and Multiple Linear Regression were implemented using the Diabetes dataset to predict disease progression. The main difference between the two approaches is the number of independent variables used for prediction. In Simple Linear Regression, BMI was selected as the independent variable, while disease progression was used as the target variable. Therefore, the simple model attempts to identify a linear relationship between BMI and disease progression. In contrast, Multiple Linear Regression uses all the available input features in the dataset to predict the target variable. This allows the model to consider information from several characteristics simultaneously rather than depending only on BMI.

The dataset was divided into training and testing sets. The training data was used to train both regression models, while the testing data was used to evaluate their performance on previously unseen observations. This provides a way to understand how well the trained models can make predictions on new data.

The performance of both models was evaluated using Mean Squared Error (MSE) and R² Score. MSE measures the average squared difference between the actual target values and the values predicted by the model. A smaller MSE indicates that the predicted values are closer to the actual values and therefore represents smaller prediction errors. The R² Score indicates how much of the variation in disease progression can be explained by the model. A higher R² Score generally indicates that the model explains a greater proportion of the variation in the target variable.

Simple Linear Regression provides a straightforward way to study the relationship between BMI and disease progression. Since it uses only one feature, the model is relatively simple and easy to understand. However, disease progression may be associated with several features present in the dataset. Multiple Linear Regression addresses this by considering all the available input features simultaneously. As a result, it can capture relationships involving features other than BMI.

The final comparison is made using the MSE and R² values obtained from both models. If the Multiple Linear Regression model produces a lower MSE and a higher R² Score than the Simple Linear Regression model, it indicates that using the additional features has improved the model's predictive performance on the test data. On the other hand, the actual results obtained from the experiment should be considered before drawing a conclusion about the relative performance of the two models.

Overall, the experiment demonstrates the difference between using a single feature and using multiple features for regression. Simple Linear Regression helps understand the relationship between BMI and disease progression, whereas Multiple Linear Regression considers the combined contribution of all available input features. Comparing their MSE and R² Scores provides a quantitative basis for understanding how the choice of input features affects prediction performance.
